<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thane</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sthanaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thane is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the Thane creek just north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>. Known in medieval inscriptions as Sthanaka, it was a centre of the Silahara kings of the North Konkan and a port visited by Arab traders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>. Later held by the Portuguese and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, it was the terminus of India's first passenger railway from Bombay in 1853.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thane.jpg" alt="Thane" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mehulsg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hiranandaniestate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Thane emerged as an urban area under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silahara-dynasty">Shilahara dynasty</a>, which ruled the North Konkan between 800 and 1265 CE. Its names in inscriptions include Srī-sthānaka, Sthānaka and Srī-sthāna. A Chinchani inscription of 1053 CE, issued by Vija-ranaka of the Modha family, states that brahmanas from Srī-sthāna settled at Samyana in his territory. A Shilahara inscription of 1094 CE calls Srī-sthānaka a velakula, a harbour that was an important centre for import and export. Early medieval Arab sources give the name as Thāna, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fida">Abulfeda</a> knew it as Kukin Tana.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> chronicle, the Mahikavatichi Bakhar, written between the 15th and 17th centuries and of doubtful accuracy, tells that in the 12th century a general named Balkrishn-rao Somvanshi captured Thane from the Shilahara king Yashwant-rao. In the 13th century, according to the same text, Nagar-shah, the ruler of Gandevi, took the area, and it later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas of Devagiri</a> and back to Nagar-shah as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. It was then ruled in turn by local families, the Muzaffarids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and the Portuguese.</p><p>In the 17th century the Thane area was contested between the Portuguese, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> captured it from the Mughals in 1675. The Portuguese regained control and began building a fort in 1734, but the Marathas captured the place in 1737. At the start of the First Anglo-Maratha War the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> occupied Salsette Island and Thana Fort; the island was recaptured by the forces of Haripant Phadke and Tukoji Holkar, but after the Treaty of Salbai of 1782 both the fort and Salsette were under British control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Thane, spelled Thana until 1996, is a city in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It lies in the north-east of Salsette Island, neighbours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> and belongs to the Mumbai Metropolitan Region. It is the headquarters of Thane district, and the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 1,886,941 inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> is the main language, with Hindi widely understood, and some East Indian families in the Khatri ward still speak Portuguese. About 1,800 of India's Jews live in the city.</p><p>On 16 April 1853 a passenger train service was inaugurated between Bori Bunder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and Thane, a distance of 34 km, hauled by three locomotives named Sahib, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>. When the line was extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan">Kalyan</a> in May 1854, the Thane viaducts, the first railway bridges, were built over Thane creek.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thane” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Silaharas of North Konkan were established around 800 when the Rashtrakuta dynasty appointed Kapardin I to govern the coastal strip around <b>Thane</b>, Mumbai and Raigad.</blockquote><a href="/state/silaharas-of-north-konkan">Silaharas of North Konkan</a></li></ul></section>
